nchanged after format? Yes likely tests assert record.msg == original. The save/restore approach passes as long as restore in finally.\n\nAlternative cleaner: temporarily replace record.getMessage? Can't set attribute on instance? You can — record.getMessage = lambda: truncated? LogRecord instances allow attribute assignment (no __slots__). Actually logging.LogRecord doesn't define __slots__, so yes. But super().format calls self.formatMessage(record) which uses record.message. So set record.message after getMessage... it's overwritten. Setting record.getMessage lambda is hacky too.\n\nBetter: implement our own formatting that mirrors base but with truncated message:\n\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    full_message = record.getMessage()\n    displayed = full_message\n    limit = self.max_message_chars\n    if limit is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        displayed = _truncate_message(full_message, limit)\n    record.message = displayed\n    ...\n```\n\nStill need to avoid super's getMessage. Approach: override getMessage via temporary swap as said. Hmm, maybe cleanest: compute record.message before, then don't call super().format(record) but replicate:\n\nActually we can do:\n\n```python\nsaved = record.msg, record.args\ntry:\n    if truncation needed:\n        record.msg = truncated\n        record.args = None\n    msg = super().format(record)\nfinally:\n    record.msg, record.args = saved\n    record.message = full_message  # restore\n```\n\nWait for no-truncation case, super sets record.message = full message (same as original). Fine.\n\nBut subtle: multi-line prefix logic uses `record.message` and msg.find(record.message): after super().format with truncated msg, record.message is truncated message, and msg contains it. Good. For no truncation, unchanged.\n\nAlso exception text: exc_info appended by super().format, not affected by msg truncation. Good.\n\nEdge: when truncation with args and fmt uses %(message)s